## Context

SPEC.md §5.2.5: "The conductor is the only writer, and the segmenter... It
runs seg/1 (§5.2.2) as a streaming state machine over the text windows the
worker forwards: it closes entries at structural boundaries..., cuts the
passages inside each entry as deterministic token windows over text it is
already holding, and commits slab, entry and passage rows as entries
close." Ticket 0553 (tranche 3, merged as PR #156 pending review) built
the worker side of this: `document-stream.ts` forwards windows via a
callback, and its own ticket log discloses plainly "windows have no
consumer until the segmenter exists" — this ticket is that consumer.

Two things exist independently and don't yet meet: seg/1 (ticket 0028 and
its children 0557-0564, the segmentation *algorithm*, itself blocked on
0502's spec propagation) and the schema to write into (ticket 0556, the
`entries`/`slabs`/`passages` tables, in flight). Neither ticket owns the
wiring between them — 0028's exit criteria stop at "seg/1 runnable over
flat /fulltext text, emitting entries + confidence"; 0556's are explicit
that "actually writing rows from a running pipeline" is tranches 4/5's
job, not its own. This ticket is that missing middle: the conductor-side
loop that calls seg/1 as windows arrive and commits what it returns.

Whether this subsumes part of 0554's "chunk worker" action, or whether
0554's chunk worker is a distinct post-segmentation step, is genuinely
unclear from the ticket texts as written (SPEC's own description has
segmentation cut passages directly — token windows — with no separate
"chunking" step named). Resolve this at execution time by reading 0554's
current text and this ticket's actual output shape; do not guess now.
Flag it in the log either way so the record says which reading was taken.

## Relevant files

- `SPEC.md` §5.2.2 — `entries`/`slabs`/`passages` shapes (already built by
  0556); the chunking/passage-budget rule (120 min / 48 overlap tokens,
  `budget = min(500, modelMax) − specialTokens − count(passagePrefix)`,
  heading path charged to the same budget and dropped rather than
  truncated past a quarter of it); slab gzip ≤ 1 MiB, `content_hash`.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.5 — the conductor-as-segmenter paragraph quoted above in
  full; "peak memory is one window plus the segmenter's own state" and
  "the conductor never materializes a whole document" — both testable
  properties, not just prose.
- Fork `src/features/search/conductor/document-stream.ts` (tranche 3) —
  the window-forwarding source this ticket consumes. Read its actual
  callback shape; don't assume one.
- Fork wherever ticket 0028 lands seg/1's own module and interface — read
  it fresh when it exists; this ticket's Actions below describe the
  integration shape, not seg/1's internals.
- Fork `src/features/search/conductor/ledger.ts` (0556's additions) — the
  tables this ticket's commits land in.

## Actions

1. Call seg/1 as text windows arrive from the extract worker's forwarding
   channel (`document-stream.ts` or whatever 0553 actually shipped —
   verify against the merged code, not this description).
2. Implement the streaming state machine: accumulate windows, detect
   structural boundaries via seg/1, close entries at accepted boundaries,
   cut passages inside each closed entry as deterministic token windows
   respecting §5.2.2's budget rule.
3. Commit slab, entry, and passage rows as entries close — one slab per
   closed unit of source text (gzip ≤ 1 MiB, `content_hash` computed),
   entries and passages referencing it per the schema 0556 built.
4. Bound peak memory to one window plus segmenter state — this is a
   tested property (see Test), not a comment.
5. Never materialize a whole document: assert directly (a test, or an
   invariant check) that no code path in this loop holds a full
   document's text in memory at once — this is what makes the "44,9 MB
   attachment inside the query-serving process" failure mode structurally
   impossible, per §5.2.9's arithmetic that motivates it.

## Test

- Red first: feed a multi-window synthetic document (extend tranche 1's
  synthetic library with real segmentable text — a small multi-heading
  document is enough) through the streaming state machine; assert the
  committed entries/slabs/passages match what segmenting the same text
  non-streaming (all at once) would produce — the streaming property must
  not change the result, only how it's computed.
- A memory-bound test: construct a document whose total text would not
  fit in a small fixed budget if buffered whole, and assert the streaming
  path completes within that budget (e.g. process-level RSS delta, or a
  more direct unit-level proof that no accumulator ever holds more than a
  bounded number of windows).

## Verification

- [ ] Entries close at seg/1's structural boundaries — reuse seg/1's own
      fixtures (ticket 0028) rather than inventing parallel ones
- [ ] Passages respect the token-budget geometry from §5.2.2, including
      the heading-path-charged-to-budget rule
- [ ] Slab gzip ≤ 1 MiB per slab; `content_hash` verifiable by the
      gunzip-slice-verify-fingerprint contract §5.2.2 states for snippets
- [ ] Peak-memory-one-window property demonstrated, not asserted in prose
- [ ] Positive control: a deliberately broken boundary detector (e.g. one
      that never closes an entry) fails the streaming-vs-non-streaming
      equivalence test before the test suite is trusted

## Invariants

- v1 (`search-index.sqlite` and everything outside `conductor/`) untouched.
- The conductor remains the sole writer — no other process writes
  `entries`/`slabs`/`passages`.
- Upstream tests green throughout.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Streaming segmentation integration merged on a fork branch
- [ ] `entries`/`slabs`/`passages` populated correctly from real (fixture)
      text, end to end, through the actual window-forwarding path
- [ ] The memory-bound property has a test, and it passes
- [ ] The 0554-overlap question (Context, above) is resolved and recorded
      in this ticket's log, whichever way it goes
